(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-8-hematologic-malignancies-i-vignettes"]={"title":"Hematologic Malignancies I Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 8","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 8","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 69-year-old woman has higher-risk myelodysplastic syndrome with marrow blasts that remain below the leukemia threshold. Which treatment is used for higher-risk disease?","choices":["Chemotherapy or hypomethylating agents","Erythropoiesis-stimulating agent","Tyrosine kinase inhibitor therapy","Phlebotomy and hydroxyurea"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Treatment is highly dependent on blood counts, risk status and mutations, and higher-risk myelodysplastic syndrome is treated with chemotherapy or hypomethylating agents. Very high-risk disease overlaps substantially with acute myeloid leukemia. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 76-year-old man with newly diagnosed acute myeloid leukemia asks what share of patients his age are alive 5 years after diagnosis. Which estimate is accurate?","choices":["About 5%","About 25%","About 48%","About 90%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Survival is lowest for patients over 70 years old, only about 5% at 5 years, and for those with adverse-risk or secondary disease. At 76 he falls in that group. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man with intermediate-risk acute myeloid leukemia has achieved remission after intensive chemotherapy. Which next step is typically used for him?","choices":["Allogeneic stem cell transplant","Autologous stem cell transplant","Stop treatment and observe","Switch to a tyrosine kinase inhibitor"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Regardless of the initial approach, allogeneic stem cell transplant is typically used for most intermediate- and high-risk patients if they are able to achieve remission. He is intermediate-risk and in remission. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman has very high-risk myelodysplastic syndrome with elevated blasts that remain below the leukemia threshold. Which outcome is she at greatest risk of within 5 years?","choices":["Progression to acute myeloid leukemia","Progression to polycythemia vera","Progression to lymphoblastic leukemia","Spontaneous recovery of counts"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Very high-risk myelodysplastic syndrome has a median overall survival of 9 months and more than an 80% risk of developing acute myeloid leukemia within 5 years. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 49, 51"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has polycythemia vera with a hematocrit of 60% (normal 41-50%) and increased blood viscosity. Which complication is he at risk for?","choices":["Thrombosis","Hypercalcemia","Pancytopenia","Tumor lysis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The complications of polycythemia vera are thrombosis, splenomegaly and bleeding. Overproduction of red cells increases blood viscosity, which drives the clotting risk in this man with a hematocrit of 60%. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 9-year-old girl has pallor, petechiae and a white blood cell count of 62,000 per microliter (normal 4,500-13,500) with circulating blasts. Acute lymphoblastic leukemia is suspected. Which testing confirms the diagnosis and shows whether it is B-cell or T-cell?","choices":["Lumbar puncture for spinal fluid cells","Peripheral smear for Auer rods","Flow cytometry and bone marrow biopsy","Chest X-ray for a mediastinal mass"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Diagnosis is confirmed by flow cytometry and bone marrow biopsy. Flow cytometry identifies the leukemia's markers and its B-cell or T-cell nature; the biopsy shows the degree of marrow infiltration and allows chromosome and mutation testing. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man has enlarged neck lymph nodes, night sweats and weight loss. The lymph node biopsy shows few malignant Reed-Sternberg cells among numerous immune effector cells. Which lymphoma group does this fit?","choices":["Lymphoblastic lymphoma","Hodgkin lymphoma","B-cell non-Hodgkin lymphoma","T-cell non-Hodgkin lymphoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hodgkin lymphoma, about 10% of all lymphomas, is characterized by few malignant cells (Reed-Sternberg cells) with numerous immune effector cells. His enlarged nodes, night sweats and weight loss are the usual lymphoma manifestations. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman with a hematologic malignancy has her own stem cells collected, then receives high-dose chemotherapy, and the collected cells are given back to rescue her marrow. Which procedure is this?","choices":["Allogeneic stem cell transplant","Emergent leukapheresis","Red blood cell transfusion","Autologous stem cell transplant"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An autologous stem cell transplant (\"stem cell rescue\") uses the person's own stem cells as part of the transplantation process, often to rescue high-dose chemotherapy. She received her own collected cells back after high-dose chemotherapy. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 12, 35, 48"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman treated with alkylating chemotherapy for breast cancer years ago now has acute myeloid leukemia. How does her history affect the classification and outlook of her leukemia?","choices":["No change from primary leukemia","Secondary leukemia with poor outcomes","Favorable-risk leukemia, likely cured","Promyelocytic leukemia, cured in most"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acute myeloid leukemia that is therapy-related (after treatment of a prior cancer), or that arises from another myeloid neoplasm, is collectively called secondary acute myeloid leukemia and is associated with poor outcomes (about 10% overall). — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 32, 38, 41, 43"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man with chronic phase chronic myelogenous leukemia has taken a tyrosine kinase inhibitor for 3 months. His BCR-ABL1 (fusion gene) level is 4%. How should this milestone response be classified?","choices":["Warning","Failure","Treatment-free remission","Optimal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. At 3 months a BCR-ABL1 (fusion gene) level below 10% is optimal and above 10% is a warning; there is no failure category at 3 months. His 4% is below 10%, so his response is optimal. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with newly diagnosed acute myeloid leukemia is fit, with an Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group performance status of 0 and no other illnesses. Which initial treatment fits her?","choices":["Tyrosine kinase inhibitor alone","Erythropoiesis-stimulating agent","Intensive cytarabine plus anthracycline","Hypomethylating agent plus venetoclax"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. For fit patients, acute myeloid leukemia is treated with intensive chemotherapy, often cytarabine plus an anthracycline. She is fully active with no other illness, so she can receive the intensive approach. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman's primary care workup finds marrow fibrosis with excess myeloid proliferation and splenomegaly, consistent with myelofibrosis. Which referral is appropriate?","choices":["Surgeon for spleen removal","Hematologist at diagnosis","Gastroenterologist for satiety","Hematologist if it later worsens"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Myelofibrosis should be referred to a hematologist upon diagnosis. It is the most aggressive myeloproliferative neoplasm, with high-risk patients often surviving less than 2 years. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman has a hematocrit of 56% (normal 36-46%), headaches and itching after warm baths. Polycythemia vera is suspected. Which mutation is it commonly the result of?","choices":["BCR-ABL1 (fusion gene)","Translocation of 15 and 17","Hyperdiploidy","JAK2 (Janus kinase 2)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Polycythemia vera commonly occurs as a result of a JAK2 (Janus kinase 2) mutation. Her high hematocrit with pruritus after warm baths is its classic picture. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 34-year-old healthy man with chronic phase chronic myelogenous leukemia has failed to respond to tyrosine kinase inhibitors, showing resistance despite treatment. Which next step is used for him?","choices":["Stop therapy and observe","Phlebotomy and hydroxyurea","Allogeneic stem cell transplant","Autologous stem cell transplant"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Allogeneic stem cell transplant is used in young healthy patients who have resistance to tyrosine kinase inhibitors or advanced-stage chronic myelogenous leukemia. He is 34, healthy and resistant. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with newly diagnosed acute myeloid leukemia asks what in his history might have raised his risk. Which item is a recognized risk factor for this leukemia?","choices":["Being female","Age under 5 years in the past","Decades of petroleum refinery work","Childhood Epstein-Barr infection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Chemical exposures, including benzene, petroleum and certain chemotherapies (alkylators), are risk factors for acute myeloid leukemia, along with prior high-dose radiation, human immunodeficiency virus, genetic syndromes, age over 60 and male sex. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 26-year-old man has Philadelphia chromosome-negative acute lymphoblastic leukemia (no 9;22 translocation). Which regimen is usually given at his age?","choices":["Arsenic trioxide with retinoic acid","Pediatric-like intensive chemotherapy","Low-intensity hypomethylating therapy","Tyrosine kinase inhibitor plus chemotherapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. For Philadelphia chromosome-negative disease, pediatric-like regimens are usually given to patients younger than 40, as he is. They are intensive chemotherapy with induction, consolidation and maintenance phases, steroids and often targeted agents. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man has newly diagnosed acute lymphoblastic leukemia, and cytogenetic testing is sent. Which result is most commonly found in adults with this disease?","choices":["Excess copies of a chromosome (hyperdiploidy)","Translocation of chromosomes 15 and 17","Janus kinase 2 mutation","BCR-ABL1 (fusion gene) from a 9;22 translocation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In adults the most common mutation is the BCR-ABL1 (fusion gene) mutation caused by translocation of chromosomes 9 and 22, the Philadelphia chromosome. Its presence changes treatment, which then adds a tyrosine kinase inhibitor to chemotherapy. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has newly diagnosed chronic myelogenous leukemia with a white blood cell count of 140,000 per microliter (normal 4,500-11,000). Her marrow shows marked granulocytic proliferation with blasts at 2% of cells. Which phase is she in?","choices":["Accelerated phase","Blast phase","Treatment-free remission","Chronic phase"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The chronic (stable) phase is the early proliferative phase with no or few blasts, under 5%; her 2% fits. It carries an excellent prognosis and makes up 85-90% of all presentations, treated with a tyrosine kinase inhibitor. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man has marked leukocytosis. His bone marrow biopsy shows proliferation of mature malignant white blood cells rather than immature progenitor cells. Which category of leukemia does this describe?","choices":["Lymphoma","Plasma cell disorder","Chronic leukemia","Acute leukemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Chronic leukemias involve proliferation of mature malignant white blood cells, as his marrow shows. Acute leukemias, by contrast, arise from more immature progenitor white cells and cause rapid proliferation of that clone. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with myelodysplastic syndrome bathes and dresses himself and is up and about for most of the day, but fatigue now keeps him from any work, even light housework or his part-time desk job. Which Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group performance status grade fits him?","choices":["Grade 1","Grade 2","Grade 3","Grade 4"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. He is ambulatory and capable of all self-care (bathing, dressing) and up and about more than half of waking hours, but unable to carry out any work activities, including light housework or desk work. That row is grade 2. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman has acute lymphoblastic leukemia, and cytogenetics show the Philadelphia chromosome (translocation of chromosomes 9 and 22). Which treatment approach fits?","choices":["Erythropoiesis-stimulating agent","Tyrosine kinase inhibitor plus chemotherapy","Hypomethylating agent plus venetoclax","Arsenic trioxide with retinoic acid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Philadelphia chromosome-positive acute lymphoblastic leukemia is treated with a tyrosine kinase inhibitor plus chemotherapy. Her translocation of chromosomes 9 and 22 makes her disease Philadelphia chromosome-positive, which sets this plan. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 27, 40, 43, 48"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with myelodysplastic syndrome has neutropenia (absolute neutrophil count 600 per microliter, normal 1,500-8,000) and recurrent infections. A myeloid growth factor is being started. Which caution applies?","choices":["Avoid red cell transfusions","Avoid warm baths and showers","Avoid aspirin with high platelets","Avoid overstimulating blasts"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Neutropenia in myelodysplastic syndrome may be treated with myeloid growth factors, but with care not to cause overstimulation of blasts. Higher-risk disease can carry blasts below 20%, and pushing them would move it toward leukemia. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"An 81-year-old woman with heart failure and chronic kidney disease has newly diagnosed acute myeloid leukemia. She is capable of only limited self-care and spends most of the day in a chair, and her team judges her unfit for intensive chemotherapy. Which initial treatment fits her?","choices":["Immediate allogeneic stem cell transplant","Hypomethylating agent with or without venetoclax","Intensive cytarabine plus anthracycline","Arsenic trioxide with retinoic acid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Unfit patients receive low-intensity therapy, frequently a hypomethylating agent with or without venetoclax. At 81 with heart failure, kidney disease and limited self-care she is unfit; fitness often dictates prognosis in older patients. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 40, 10, 43"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man has fatigue and bruising. Bone marrow biopsy shows lymphoid blasts making up 36% of marrow cells, with diffuse marrow and peripheral blood involvement and no nodal or extranodal mass. How should this finding be classified?","choices":["Myelodysplastic syndrome","Chronic myelogenous leukemia","Acute lymphoblastic leukemia","Aggressive lymphoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Acute lymphoblastic leukemia is defined by blast cells of lymphoid origin at 20% or more of the marrow; his 36% clears that threshold. Diffuse marrow and blood involvement without a nodal mass is the leukemia pattern rather than an aggressive lymphoma. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 19-year-old college student has sore throat, fever and fatigue, and his blood smear shows atypical lymphocytosis. Which test separates a reactive lymphocytosis from acute lymphoblastic leukemia?","choices":["Chest X-ray for a mass","Lumbar puncture","BCR-ABL1 (fusion gene) testing","Flow cytometry for clonality"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An atypical lymphocytosis can be seen with infections, including mononucleosis and pertussis. Flow cytometry differentiates these from acute lymphoblastic leukemia based on the clonal nature of the leukemia. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman has no symptoms. A routine blood count shows platelets of 1,150,000 per microliter (normal 150,000-450,000) with normal hemoglobin and white count. Testing finds a Janus kinase pathway gene mutation. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Essential thrombocythemia","Polycythemia vera","Chronic myelogenous leukemia","Myelofibrosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Essential thrombocythemia often arises from Janus kinase pathway gene mutations that cause overproduction of platelets, and it is mostly asymptomatic. Her isolated very high platelet count with normal red and white cells fits. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 51, 54, 59"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man with pneumonia has a white blood cell count of 28,000 per microliter (normal 4,500-11,000). He has no splenomegaly, red cell morphology is normal, and polymerase chain reaction for BCR-ABL1 (fusion gene) is negative. How should his leukocytosis be classified?","choices":["Reactive leukocytosis","Chronic phase leukemia","Accelerated phase leukemia","Myelofibrosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Reactive leukocytosis can be seen with infections and is distinguished from chronic myelogenous leukemia by no splenomegaly, a white count usually below 50,000 and a negative BCR-ABL1 (fusion gene) test. His pneumonia, count of 28,000 and negative test fit. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man has no symptoms, but a routine blood count shows a white blood cell count of 186,000 per microliter (normal 4,500-11,000), mostly granulocytes and myelocytes, with platelets of 620,000 per microliter (normal 150,000-450,000) and very few blasts. His spleen is enlarged. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acute myeloid leukemia","Reactive leukocytosis","Essential thrombocythemia","Chronic myelogenous leukemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. About half of patients are asymptomatic and found through leukocytosis on a blood count. Marked leukocytosis (often above 100,000) of granulocytes, elevated platelets, very low blasts and splenomegaly fit chronic phase chronic myelogenous leukemia, median age 45-55. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 59, 61"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has gum bleeding, nosebleeds and widespread bruising. Labs show pancytopenia with prolonged clotting times, low fibrinogen and disseminated intravascular coagulation. Marrow shows arrested maturation at the promyelocyte, and cytogenetics show a translocation of chromosomes 15 and 17. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Chronic myelogenous leukemia","Essential thrombocythemia","Acute promyelocytic leukemia","Acute lymphoblastic leukemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Acute promyelocytic leukemia is a rare acute myeloid leukemia subtype caused by arrested differentiation at the promyelocyte and a 15;17 translocation fusing the PML-RARA (promyelocytic leukemia-retinoic acid receptor alpha) genes. It often presents emergently with disseminated intravascular coagulation and bleeding. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with newly diagnosed acute myeloid leukemia still walks without help and works her office job, but she has stopped hiking and heavy yard work because they exhaust her. Which Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group performance status grade fits her?","choices":["Grade 0","Grade 1","Grade 2","Grade 3"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. She is restricted in physically strenuous activity (hiking, heavy yard work) but is ambulatory and still carries out work of a sedentary nature at her office job: that row is grade 1. Fitness grading of this kind often helps dictate prognosis, particularly in older patients. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 10"}]};
